# Water systems' controls wiped through a shared remote-support platform: Minot, North Dakota
variant "Minot, North Dakota"
    summary "An AI agent wipes the controls of the water systems of Minot, North Dakota at once, through the
        remote-support platform of the OT integrator that they share: Minot and NAWS."
    scope "The people that the water systems of Minot, North Dakota serve, the hospitals that those systems
        supply and the businesses that need public water there."

minot: Utility
    "City of Minot. Its ID in the federal inventory is ND5100660."
    title "Minot"
    let buys = []
    let sells = [minot_to_naws]
    name = "Minot"
    demand = 10.2021 Mgal/day  @minot_demand
    nameplate = 18 Mgal/day  @minot_nameplate
    storage = 14 Mgal  @minot_storage
    bought = 0 Mgal/day  @minot_bought
    sold = 717000 gal/day  @minot_sold
    va_rate = 425105.14 USD2025/h  @minot_va_rate
    pump_disabled = 0.7  @depth
    manual_share = 0.8  @manual
    activation_delay = 1 h  @manual
    controls_load = 400 work_h  @work
    field_load = 80 work_h  @work

naws: Utility
    "Northwest Area Water Supply (NAWS), North Dakota. Its ID in the federal inventory is ND5101687."
    title "NAWS"
    let buys = [minot_to_naws]
    let sells = []
    name = "NAWS"
    demand = 0 Mgal/day  @naws_demand
    nameplate = 0 Mgal/day  @naws_nameplate
    storage = 8.5 Mgal  @naws_storage
    bought = 0 Mgal/day  @naws_bought
    sold = 1.24789 Mgal/day  @naws_sold
    va_rate = 0 USD2025/h  @naws_va_rate

minot_to_naws: Tie
    "Minot sells water to NAWS."
    let seller = minot
    let buyer = naws
    capacity = 1439300 gal/day  @minot_to_naws_capacity

minot_homes: Households
    "The people that Minot serves."
    title "Minot: homes"
    let utility = minot
    population = 48743 people  @minot_homes_population

minot_hospitals: Hospitals
    "The hospital that Minot supplies: Trinity Hospitals."
    title "Minot: hospitals"
    let utility = minot
    name = "Minot"
    facilities = 1 facilities  @minot_hospitals_facilities

notes:
    limitations:
        naws-pipeline: "About half of Minot's own water runs through the NAWS pumps and pipeline, but the
            model counts it as Minot's own delivery. A stop of the NAWS pumps or pipeline would also cut those
            city connections, so the model can show a smaller loss for the city than in
            fact."  @minot_to_naws_capacity
        peak-statement: "Minot's peak of 13 Mgal a day rests on a general statement of 2020, and later reports
            are lower. So the peak here can be high, and Minot's stored water can run out sooner here than in
            fact."  @minot_demand
    verification:
        research: "variants/minot/evidence/research.md"

record minot_demand: assumption A-SC43-PEAK-DAY "The highest day that a source reports is 13 million gallons,
    the lower end of the 13 to 14 million gallons a day that peak summer use often reaches (2020). The 2024
    pumped average is 6.6896 million gallons a day, so the peak ratio is 1.9433. Less the sales to NAWS at
    that ratio (0.8083 x 1.9433 = 1.5708) and about 0.717 million gallons a day to North Prairie Rural Water,
    over 1.05: (13 - 1.5708 - 0.717) / 1.05 = 10.2021."
    claims:
        E-SC43-MINOT-MINOT-PEAK
        E-SC43-MINOT-MINOT-PRODUCTION
        E-SC43-MINOT-NAWS-CONTRACT
        E-SC43-MINOT-MINOT-PRODUCTION-2012
        E-SC43-MINOT-MINOT-USE-2021

record minot_nameplate: source "The rated capacity of the one Minot water treatment plant, 18 million gallons
    a day, as the Department of Water Resources reported in 2023 and again in January 2025. The plant has only
    ground water until lake water arrives, and the wells give about 17 million gallons a day."
    claims:
        E-SC43-MINOT-MINOT-CAPACITY-2025
            datum: wtp_capacity
        E-SC43-MINOT-MINOT-CAPACITY
            datum: wtp_capacity

record minot_storage: source "The 2014 master plan gives 12.5 million gallons in the city reservoirs and
    towers; the 1.5 million-gallon southwest tower went online in 2022, so 12.5 + 1.5 = 14.0. The current city
    page lists three 3-million-gallon ground reservoirs and five elevated tanks, which with the tank volumes
    of the 2014 plan and the 2018 and 2021 reports also gives 14.0 million gallons."
    claims:
        E-SC43-MINOT-MINOT-STORAGE-2014
        E-SC43-MINOT-MINOT-SW-TOWER
        E-SC43-MINOT-MINOT-TANKS
        E-SC43-MINOT-MINOT-NORTH-TOWER

record minot_bought: source "Minot produces all its water from its own wells and plant. Lake water through
    NAWS had not begun in the period: the Biota plant was not complete in May 2026."
    claims:
        E-SC43-MINOT-NAWS-BIOTA
        E-SC43-MINOT-MINOT-CAPACITY

record minot_sold: assumption A-SC43-PEAK-DAY "About 717,000 gallons a day to North Prairie Rural Water, which
    the NAWS flow table counts inside the Minot estimated peak usage; it is taken as the peak-day sale. North
    Prairie is outside the scenario, and the rate memo of November 2025 bills its NAWS connections under the
    city rate."
    claims:
        E-SC43-MINOT-NAWS-CONTRACT
            datum: nprw_daily_use
        E-SC43-MINOT-NAWS-BUYERS

record minot_homes_population: assumption A-SC43-REACH "SDWIS federal data list 48,743 people served by Minot
    (ND5100660), the system's own report through its state."
    claims:
        E-SC43-SDWIS-ND5100660
            datum: population_served

record minot_va_rate: assumption A-SC43-ECONOMY "48,743 people served times the 2024 GDP per resident of its
    county (Ward County, North Dakota (38101): 5,093,673 thousand dollars and 68,427 people: 74,439.52 dollars
    a resident) times the price factor 1.026324, over 8,760 h: 425,105.14 USD2025/h."
    claims:
        E-SC43-SDWIS-ND5100660
        E-SC43-SDWIS-COUNTIES
        E-SC43-BEA-GDP-38101
        E-SC43-BEA-POP-38101
        E-SC43-GDP-DEFLATOR

record minot_hospitals_facilities: assumption A-SC43-HOSPITAL-JOIN "The CMS hospital list gives 1 hospital of
    the counted types whose geocoded address lies in the EPA service area of Minot."
    claims:
        E-SC43-HOSPITALS-MINOT
            datum: hospitals_supplied

record naws_demand: source "NAWS has no retail customers of its own: it delivers to nine contract customers
    and to connections that the City of Minot bills."
    claims:
        E-SC43-MINOT-NAWS-BUYERS

record naws_nameplate: source "NAWS had no treatment plant in service in the period. Phase I of the Biota
    plant was still under construction in May 2026, with substantial completion expected at the end of August
    2026, and it only pretreats lake water before final treatment at the Minot plant."
    claims:
        E-SC43-MINOT-NAWS-BIOTA
        E-SC43-MINOT-NAWS-DESIGN
        E-SC43-MINOT-MINOT-OUTSIDE-USE-2021

record naws_storage: source "Sum of the NAWS tanks with a reported volume: Lansford 4.5, Bottineau 3 and the
    Kenmare tower 1 million gallons, 8.5 in all. It is a lower bound: the tanks on Speedway Hill at Minot and
    near Berthold have no reported volume."
    claims:
        E-SC43-MINOT-NAWS-RESERVOIRS
        E-SC43-MINOT-NAWS-BOTTINEAU
        E-SC43-MINOT-NAWS-KENMARE-TOWER

record naws_bought: source "NAWS buys all its water from Minot, which is in the scenario (the tie). No lake
    water flowed in the period."
    claims:
        E-SC43-MINOT-NAWS-BIOTA
        E-SC43-MINOT-MINOT-PRODUCTION

record naws_sold: assumption A-SC43-PEAK-DAY "The nine NAWS contract customers used 295,837,470 gallons in
    2024, 0.8083 million gallons a day. No source reports a NAWS highest day, so the family median ratio
    1.54385 applies: 0.8083 x 1.54385 = 1.2479."
    claims:
        E-SC43-MINOT-MINOT-PRODUCTION

record naws_va_rate: assumption A-SC43-ECONOMY "NAWS (ND5101687) is a wholesale system with no retail
    customers: SDWIS lists 0 people, so its own homes and businesses add no value added; its buyers carry
    theirs."
    claims:
        E-SC43-SDWIS-ND5101687

record minot_to_naws_capacity: source "The daily amounts of Amendment 2 to the interim water supply agreement,
    for all NAWS customers, total 1,439,300 gallons a day. The NAWS pumps at the High Service Pump Station can
    move 4,000 gallons a minute (5.76 million gallons a day), but they also carry the city's own water through
    the NAWS meter, so the contract total is the capacity for the NAWS customers."
    claims:
        E-SC43-MINOT-NAWS-CONTRACT
            datum: interim_flows_amendment_2
        E-SC43-MINOT-NAWS-PUMPS
